just remember when someone asks you if  you're a god you say yes ghostbusters  2016 and yes I already had this so I  have now finally seen it before I get  into the review proper a couple things I  want to say ahead of time if you follow  this channel at all you know I've been  extremely nervous about this movie  partly because the original Ghostbusters  is my favorite movie of all time I was  gonna be really nervous about any reboot  remake or even proper sequel to this  thing but before I get into this review  there's a couple of things that I want  to say up front and because they may  color what you think of my opinion and I  just want to get them out of the way you  may have seen my earlier video where I  called this up for being rebooted than a  continuation saying I there was nothing  that I had seen that made it clear to me  why this could not be a continuation now  honestly that's still the case I was not  able to sit there and not compare it to  the original in some cases point for  point which in my mind brings up one of  the criticisms that I'm gonna say  legitimately which is that it spends too  much of its first half kind of rehashing  the same points but I mean oh I could  not divorce I could not sit there and  just let it wash over me as its own  thing now with that prejudice noted up  front it's fine I mean honestly that's  it it's fine it's not bad I still can't  say that I really liked it but again  keep in mind my prejudice is taking  completely unto itself it's fine and  fine movies generally break into one of  two categories they're either just  mediocre across the board which would be  something like say x-men apocalypse  where there's nothing really wrong with  it but nothing really stands out as  being great either or there are fine  movies that have things that are good  about them but most of them tend to be  offset somewhat by things that aren't so  good about them and that for me is this  movie and that can sometimes be the more  frustrating option because you see stuff  that works you know like why couldn't  you just keep doing that stuff and not  doing the stuff that didn't work but  that's what happens this is what we go  so what's the plot the plot it the plot  is the first Ghostbusters pretty much  it's not a point for point remake but it  does spend a lot of time rehashing the  same major plot points in terms of  then getting kicked out of academia than  starting up the business on their own  nobody believing them the manifestations  are leading to something bigger etc etc  etc and really eventually I did sort of  settle in and start actually enjoying  the movie it hit a point where I felt  like it was finally doing its own thing  more than just rehashing the old scenes  in with this new style of humor which  I'll come back to in a bit and you know  it finally eventually felt like its own  legitimate story and I checked the time  on my phone well at the point that I  finally felt that and that was an hour  and 15 minutes into the movie so for the  first hour 15 I was kind of annoyed not  actively annoyed like I said it's not  bad it's not terrible but it was going  over all the beats and the points in the  original and that's that's not good  enough and again that brings up why I  feel like this should not have been a  reboot they wouldn't have had to repeat  so many of the same points if they had  built off the old continuity and they  didn't have to reestablish all this junk  they wouldn't have had to go through the  rules of the tech and the ghosts and  they did all they had all they would  have had to do was establish these  characters but eh anyway I'll try I'll  try out to get on that too much I did  mention that the style of the humor is  not to my liking and that's just a  reality of sort of one of the prevalent  and popular ways of doing comedy in  films these days it's what I call  awkwardness comedy and I don't like  awkwardness comedy and awkwardness  comedy it tends to be that the setup for  what the joke is you see it coming and  but the way that they try and milk  humour out of it is just how long  they're willing it to just let it sit  there and let the moment be yeah okay  and some people find that funny and  that's fine I don't I like my humor much  more guerilla style I like it in and out  I like it I like it when the plot is  moving and you find a place to do jokes  I have gone on record and other places  saying that you could strip every single  joke out of the original Ghostbusters  and it would still be a really  interesting movie partly because no  scene exists solely for the purpose of  telling you joke every scene is the  scene you would still need even if  was a movie with no humor in it because  it establishes things about the  characters and moves the plot forward  there are scenes there are chunks in  this version of ghostbusters that exists  for no reason other to generate jokes  and I'm not gonna say you can't do that  but what that means is if the joke falls  flat then the entire scene just hurts  but if you can integrate it into the  story that even if the jokes fall flat  at least the stories at least the scene  still served a purpose I mean I think  mighty example in this one would be  Kevin who's played by Chris Hemsworth is  being interviewed for the secretary  position and that interview scene goes  on a while and it tells us almost  nothing we didn't already figure out  about Kevin or that the movie wouldn't  reiterate later and it has nothing to it  just it's there to generate jokes and if  those jokes don't lame for you it's a  painful scene but look once this thing  hit the our 15 mark and it started with  that was the point at which they figured  out the the leylines and figured out  what was gonna happen next the thing  kind of kicked into high gear for me at  that point it felt more like its own  thing and I was able to get into it and  they kicked in the music they used the  the original theme quite a bit I noticed  that the new song that came out was only  showed up once the classic Ghostbusters  theme is quite prevalent and I like that  but you know the theme is kicking and I  where I really I love it when they are  in time you know it's funny I was I was  afraid I was gonna have to label this a  spoiler review but if you've been  watching the trailers everything I'm  about to tell you've already seen so  again this is another case of the  trailers showing too much but when  they're in Times Square you know kicking  the butts of all these ghosts if that is  a really good section and that whole  segment and the movie has its own life  and its own energy at that point and I  was genuinely enjoying it I suppose I'll  talk about some of the specific  characters as well basically in terms of  the core for it was a very mixed bag for  me first up you've got Melissa McCarthy  who's fine in it but she's not doing  anything here you didn't already see her  doing spy there's Kristen Wiig now and  unfortunately Kristen Wiig is sort of  Luke  lene of that awkwardness comedy that I  talked about and she doesn't very well  but I don't like it as a comedy style so  I did not like her  kate mckinnon was good and fun and  energetic and it didn't completely click  for me it felt a little too many sort of  affectations and it felt a little put on  but she did it well as she was fun to  have there here was the big surprise my  favorite of the four was Leslie Jones I  I was shocked by that because I hated  her in the trailers I she I thought she  was gonna be the worst thing about this  but honestly she's my favorite one  because she feels the most natural she  has a couple of overblown moments like  the whole the power Patty compels you  thing but aside from that and maybe one  other time her jokes feel organically  coming out of the character it feels  like this is something that she's she  would have just said she's not trying to  milk a joke this is just what that  character would say and sometimes it  happens to be funny and that's very much  in keeping with the style of comedy that  the original Ghostbusters have that I  love so much  the effects are fine they're very CGI  but again changing times new technology  I still prefer the old stuff but again  it could be nostalgia the one other  thing I feel the need to plan is that  the the original cast cameos all of them  painful they were shoehorned in they  weren't funny and I'm including Slimer  and Stay Puft Marshmallow Man on those  cameo lists they just none of them  worked  yeah it was fine it does not ruin the  old one as far as remakes or reboots or  whatever of really good originals go in  recent years it's better than Robocop  it's better than Total Recall but it's  still not great I don't love it I  hesitate to say whether or not I like it  but again I'm not gonna call it a bad  movie here's what I'll say and this  might seem contradictory I didn't  particularly like this movie there were  things about I like but the movie  overall it was okay I do hope it gets a  sequel and the reason I hope it gets a  sequel is because I hope that will mean  that they will do a movie where the  whole thing feels like the last hour of  this movie where once they've gotten all  the setup and expository junk out of the  way it was able  just feel like its own thing and I'm  hoping that with this movie behind it if  they get to do a sequel it will get a  whole movie like that and I will be able  to just flat-out enjoy that okay I got  to wrap this up guys I am melting in  this thing because I have to turn the  fan off when I do shoot these I'm I'm  really hot in this and sadly not in the  way that I would like that to be anyways  what were your thoughts on the new  Ghostbusters whatever they were drop  something down in the comments and let's  talk about it try and keep it respectful  though I know this movie touched a lot  of hot buttons but let's stay nice if  you like my stuff give me a like give me  a subscribe check me out on Twitter  right Council of geeks give a listen to  the council of geeks podcast available  on iTunes and stitcher and until next  time this council is adjourned
 

